  "requirement": "The Habeas crate MUST have a Cargo integration test that invokes `habeas::validate()` against its own project, so its own requirements and evidence are checked recursively by `cargo test`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation: the crate MUST have a Cargo integration test that invokes `habeas::validate()` against its own project.\n\n- **Test exists:** `tests/habeas.rs`, `fn[habeas]` (lines 1–5), is a `#[test]` in `tests/`. Cargo auto-discovers it as an integration test, because `Cargo.toml` shows no `autotests = false` or custom `[[test]]` entries.\n- **Calls `validate()`:** the test body is `habeas::validate();`.\n- **Runs against its own project:** `validate()` in `src/lib.rs` (lines 32–55) starts from the current directory, which is the package root under `cargo test`. It walks up to the nearest directory holding both `Cargo.toml` and `book.toml`, then runs `validate_repository` on it. It panics if the report has errors. The habeas crate's own project is therefore checked on every `cargo test`.\n- **Marking:** the test carries `{test}` for `requirements.integration.self_test`, and the references tool reports exactly that one site.\n- **Implementation and extra behavior:** the test file is the implementation, so no separate `{impl}` is needed. The test adds no unspecified behavior.\n- **`validate()` itself:** its behavior is covered by `requirements.integration.validate`, which is not reassessed here.\n\nNo gaps found.",
